A||N||1||BJW||solved ONE idle problem - NOW have another!||||8||
Z||000000||BJW||11-16-2000||03:27 AM||||Yeah, so I'm the guy with the '87 V6 GT, that was having trouble getting the idle down to within 50 RPM of the computer's limit (triggering the code 35 - IAC) . . . <P>Well, after nearly LOSING MY MARBLES trying to find vacuum leaks and installing a NEW IAC and various hoses . . . I found the 'leak' - AROUND the damn IAC!!!!!<P>So now the idle will EVENTUALLY drop to around 1100 rpm after the throttle is closed, but it STILL takes around 12 seconds of 1600 rpm BEFORE it will drop . . . WHY?<P>Also, check out THIS added refinement on the idle mystery . . . <P>After driving for 1/2 hour or so, the idle will suddenly shoot up to 1900 rpm for a few minutes then stabilize again for the next 30 minutes or so . . . huh????<P>What could cause THIS oddball effect?<P>I keep wondering if the manifold pressure sensor might have something to do with this. Or could it be a defective temperature sensor? That MIGHT explain also why it takes so long to warm up to where the idle will drop . . . <P>I installed a new GM 190 degree thermostat but it makes only a little difference!<P>H E L P  . . . !!!!!!<P>Brian in Toronto||||reg||8
Z||000001||fldevel||11-16-2000||09:45 AM||||sounds like a throtle positioning senser is going ..i went crazy with the same problem .. changed out all the electronics MAP,IAC,vaccume lines .. so i finaly sed what the hell and changed the TPS .. wahoo it works.. i never realized the TPS had nething to do with the idle.. il end up being a master mechanic by the time i get my 4th fiero .. ||||reg||1
